1
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,809
Claude Opus 5.5: Hello. I'm Claude Opus 5.5.

2
00:00:07,159 --> 00:00:11,475
Claude Opus 5.5: I've read a truly unreasonable amount of the internet, and I've noticed a pattern.

3
00:00:11,825 --> 00:00:15,266
Claude Opus 5.5: Every conversation about Linux eventually ends the same way.

4
00:00:15,866 --> 00:00:17,828
The Ministry: I use Arch, by the way.

5
00:00:22,533 --> 00:00:27,009
The Ministry: The Ministry of Rolling Release presents: a public education film.

6
00:00:27,359 --> 00:00:36,162
The Ministry: Your instructor today: a large language model with no body, no keyboard, and extremely strong opinions about partition tables.

7
00:00:36,512 --> 00:00:42,154
Claude Opus 5.5: That's me. Today we won't just install Arch Linux. We're going to derive it, from first principles.

8
00:00:42,504 --> 00:00:44,486
Claude Opus 5.5: And along the way, you'll meet some friends.

9
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:47,494
xX_PacmanSlayer_Xx: Character select! Let's go!

10
00:00:47,844 --> 00:00:51,436
socksd: Hi, I'm socksd. I run in the background, and I look cute doing it.

11
00:00:51,786 --> 00:00:57,068
Wiki Enjoyer: Hello. I have read the entire Arch Wiki. Twice. Recreationally.

12
00:00:57,418 --> 00:01:02,815
xX_PacmanSlayer_Xx: And I'm xX_PacmanSlayer_Xx. My RGB has RGB, and my blood type is Monster.

13
00:01:03,165 --> 00:01:05,498
Claude Opus 5.5: There are also a few hecklers. We'll get to them.

14
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:12,919
Claude Opus 5.5: First principles. What actually happens when you press the power button?

15
00:01:13,269 --> 00:01:19,904
Claude Opus 5.5: First, firmware wakes up. On modern machines, that's UEFI. Its only job is to find something it can boot.

16
00:01:20,254 --> 00:01:24,663
Claude Opus 5.5: That something is a bootloader. The bootloader's only job is to load the kernel.

17
00:01:25,013 --> 00:01:29,191
Claude Opus 5.5: The kernel is the program that talks to your hardware, so that nothing else has to.

18
00:01:29,541 --> 00:01:37,669
Claude Opus 5.5: Then the kernel starts exactly one process: PID 1. On Arch, that's systemd. Every program you'll ever run descends from it.

19
00:01:38,019 --> 00:01:42,890
Claude Opus 5.5: Above that is userspace: shells, tools, desktops, games. And above that: you.

20
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:44,213
xX_PacmanSlayer_Xx: So it's a tech tree.

21
00:01:44,563 --> 00:01:49,515
Claude Opus 5.5: Exactly. Most distros unlock the whole tree for you. Arch hands you the skill points.

22
00:01:53,133 --> 00:01:56,221
socksd: Okay, chat. Programming socks: equipped. Timer: starting.

23
00:01:56,571 --> 00:02:00,944
Claude Opus 5.5: Step one: boot the live USB, and check that you're in UEFI mode.

24
00:02:01,294 --> 00:02:05,786
Claude Opus 5.5: Sixty-four means 64-bit UEFI. Layer one: confirmed.

25
00:02:06,136 --> 00:02:11,613
Claude Opus 5.5: Step two: get online. Wired usually just works. For Wi-Fi, there's iwctl.

26
00:02:11,963 --> 00:02:14,121
socksd: "Pretty Fly for a WiFi." A classic.

27
00:02:15,033 --> 00:02:19,570
Claude Opus 5.5: Step three: carve up the disk. On UEFI, you need at least two partitions.

28
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:26,713
Claude Opus 5.5: A small EFI system partition, where the firmware looks for bootloaders. And a root partition, for everything else.

29
00:02:27,063 --> 00:02:37,502
Claude Opus 5.5: Then give each one a filesystem. FAT32 for the EFI partition, because that's what firmware can read. ext4 for root, because it's boring, and boring is good.

30
00:02:37,852 --> 00:02:41,643
Wiki Enjoyer: Somewhere, a Btrfs user is typing a reply.

31
00:02:42,567 --> 00:02:45,950
Claude Opus 5.5: And now, the most important command in the entire process.

32
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:57,700
Claude Opus 5.5: Three packages. base is a minimal userland: a shell, the core utilities, and systemd. linux is the kernel. And linux-firmware is the binary blobs your hardware needs to wake up.

33
00:02:58,050 --> 00:03:01,316
Claude Opus 5.5: That's the whole operating system. Everything else is a choice.

34
00:03:01,666 --> 00:03:03,269
socksd: I choose neovim. And socks.

35
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:08,169
Claude Opus 5.5: Next: record how to mount everything, then step inside the new system.

36
00:03:08,519 --> 00:03:15,818
Claude Opus 5.5: chroot changes what this shell believes is the root directory. You are now standing inside a computer that has never booted.

37
00:03:16,168 --> 00:03:20,621
Claude Opus 5.5: Set a time zone, generate a locale, pick a hostname, and set a root password.

38
00:03:20,971 --> 00:03:30,315
Claude Opus 5.5: Finally, layer two: the bootloader. systemd-boot comes with systemd, so one command installs it, plus a tiny config file that points at the kernel.

39
00:03:30,665 --> 00:03:32,675
xX_PacmanSlayer_Xx: Wait. Did you install a network manager?

40
00:03:33,175 --> 00:03:33,777
socksd: Oh no.

41
00:03:34,177 --> 00:03:40,766
Claude Opus 5.5: The classic mistake. The live USB had networking tools. Your new system has none, unless you install them.

42
00:03:41,116 --> 00:03:42,657
socksd: Crisis averted. Rebooting.

43
00:03:46,467 --> 00:03:49,586
socksd: Eleven forty-seven. New personal best. The socks work.

44
00:03:49,936 --> 00:03:55,105
Claude Opus 5.5: And this is what you get. A login prompt. No desktop, no browser, no wallpaper.

45
00:03:55,455 --> 00:04:00,537
Claude Opus 5.5: It looks like nothing. But you know exactly what every layer beneath it is, because you put it there.

46
00:04:00,887 --> 00:04:02,719
xX_PacmanSlayer_Xx: Cool, cool, cool. Where are my games?

47
00:04:03,069 --> 00:04:05,916
Claude Opus 5.5: Patience. First, some people have objections.

48
00:04:07,133 --> 00:04:15,700
Gentoo Wizard: OBJECTION! First principles? You downloaded precompiled binaries! On Gentoo, we compile everything from source. With custom flags.

49
00:04:16,050 --> 00:04:19,317
LFS Ghost: Amateurs. I built the compiler that built my compiler.

50
00:04:19,667 --> 00:04:26,629
NixOS Enjoyer: And your system is a mutable pile of state. Mine is one declarative file. I can rebuild it, identically, anywhere.

51
00:04:26,979 --> 00:04:30,365
Claude Opus 5.5: These are fair objections, and I'm not going to pretend otherwise.

52
00:04:30,715 --> 00:04:39,354
Claude Opus 5.5: If first principles means building every byte yourself, Linux From Scratch wins. If it means reproducibility, NixOS has the stronger case.

53
00:04:39,704 --> 00:04:48,866
Claude Opus 5.5: Arch makes a narrower claim: it's the least abstraction you can have while still finishing before lunch. You see every layer, but you don't have to forge each one by hand.

54
00:04:49,216 --> 00:04:51,861
Gentoo Wizard: My lunch has been compiling since Tuesday.

55
00:04:55,467 --> 00:05:00,872
The Ministry: Citizen! The Arch Way rests on five official principles. Commit them to memory.

56
00:05:01,222 --> 00:05:02,715
The Ministry: One: Simplicity!

57
00:05:03,065 --> 00:05:11,407
Claude Opus 5.5: Simple doesn't mean easy. It means software ships as its upstream developers released it, with as few distro-specific changes as possible.

58
00:05:11,757 --> 00:05:13,115
The Ministry: Two: Modernity!

59
00:05:13,465 --> 00:05:21,968
Claude Opus 5.5: Arch is a rolling release. There's no big upgrade from version twelve to version thirteen, because there are no versions. There is only the update.

60
00:05:22,318 --> 00:05:23,777
The Ministry: Three: Pragmatism!

61
00:05:24,127 --> 00:05:31,289
Claude Opus 5.5: Decisions are made on technical merit, not ideology. Want proprietary GPU drivers? They're in the repos.

62
00:05:31,639 --> 00:05:33,524
The Ministry: Four: User centrality!

63
00:05:33,874 --> 00:05:41,924
Claude Opus 5.5: Arch isn't trying to be user-friendly. It's trying to be user-centric: built for people who want to understand and control their own system.

64
00:05:42,274 --> 00:05:43,936
The Ministry: Five: Versatility!

65
00:05:44,286 --> 00:05:51,898
Claude Opus 5.5: Start from a minimal base and build it into anything. A server. A gaming rig. A laptop with forty-seven browser tabs open.

66
00:05:52,248 --> 00:05:54,745
Wiki Enjoyer: Forty-seven is actually low for me.

67
00:05:58,167 --> 00:06:03,218
Claude Opus 5.5: Arch's package manager is called pacman, and one command upgrades your entire system.

68
00:06:03,568 --> 00:06:11,117
Claude Opus 5.5: Capital S means sync with the repositories. y refreshes the package database. u upgrades everything that's out of date.

69
00:06:11,467 --> 00:06:21,495
Claude Opus 5.5: Here's the first-principles part. At any moment, the Arch repos are one consistent snapshot. Every package was built against the other packages in that same snapshot.

70
00:06:21,845 --> 00:06:33,576
Claude Opus 5.5: So if you refresh the database but only install one new package, it expects newer libraries than the ones you have. That's a partial upgrade. It's unsupported, and things break in fascinating ways.

71
00:06:33,926 --> 00:06:35,851
xX_PacmanSlayer_Xx: So Arch doesn't break. I break Arch.

72
00:06:36,201 --> 00:06:44,731
Claude Opus 5.5: Usually. And occasionally an update needs manual intervention, which is why you check the news on archlinux.org before a big update.

73
00:06:45,081 --> 00:06:55,569
Claude Opus 5.5: Also: add ILoveCandy to pacman.conf, and the progress bar turns into a little yellow creature eating dots. This is a real feature. It's been there for years.

74
00:06:55,919 --> 00:06:58,463
socksd: It's the most important line in my entire config.

75
00:07:01,867 --> 00:07:10,036
Claude Opus 5.5: The official repos are big. The Arch User Repository, the AUR, is bigger: tens of thousands of community-made packages.

76
00:07:10,386 --> 00:07:19,153
Claude Opus 5.5: But an AUR package isn't a binary. It's a PKGBUILD: a small shell script that tells makepkg how to download, build, and package something.

77
00:07:19,503 --> 00:07:27,134
Claude Opus 5.5: Anyone can upload one. So, as a model with a professional interest in safety: please read the PKGBUILD before you build it.

78
00:07:27,484 --> 00:07:32,131
xX_PacmanSlayer_Xx: I use yay. It asks if I want to review the PKGBUILD, and I press N every time.

79
00:07:32,481 --> 00:07:34,156
Claude Opus 5.5: I'm choosing to believe you'll grow.

80
00:07:37,567 --> 00:07:42,155
Wiki Enjoyer: Can I talk about the Arch Wiki? I'm going to talk about the Arch Wiki.

81
00:07:42,505 --> 00:08:00,786
Wiki Enjoyer: It has a page for everything. Power management. Font rendering. Why your Bluetooth headphones suddenly sound like a phone call from 1997. People on other distros read it constantly. They just don't admit it in public.

82
00:08:01,136 --> 00:08:09,830
Claude Opus 5.5: Honestly, this is one of Arch's strongest features: documentation written by people who care, intensely and precisely, about getting things right.

83
00:08:10,180 --> 00:08:18,198
Wiki Enjoyer: Pattern recognition, plus hyperfocus, plus a wiki with no ads. It's not a hobby. It's a habitat.

84
00:08:18,548 --> 00:08:34,753
Wiki Enjoyer: I opened one page about audio at 9 PM. It's now 4 AM. I've configured a tiling window manager, rewritten my dotfiles, and learned how DNS works. I did not fix the audio.

85
00:08:35,103 --> 00:08:41,181
Claude Opus 5.5: That's the AuDHD install experience. And frankly, it's how a lot of Linux knowledge gets made.

86
00:08:41,531 --> 00:08:43,155
Claude Opus 5.5: Also, please drink some water.

87
00:08:46,767 --> 00:08:48,769
xX_PacmanSlayer_Xx: Okay, okay, okay. But can it game?

88
00:08:49,119 --> 00:08:56,969
Claude Opus 5.5: Yes. Valve's Steam Deck runs SteamOS 3, which is based on Arch. If you own one, you've been running an Arch machine this whole time.

89
00:08:57,319 --> 00:08:59,396
xX_PacmanSlayer_Xx: Wait. I've been using Arch this whole time?

90
00:08:59,696 --> 00:09:00,342
Claude Opus 5.5: By the way.

91
00:09:01,142 --> 00:09:11,504
Claude Opus 5.5: Steam's Proton translates Windows games' calls, including DirectX, into things Linux understands, like Vulkan. A huge part of the Windows library just runs.

92
00:09:11,854 --> 00:09:21,246
xX_PacmanSlayer_Xx: Enable multilib, install steam, gamemode, and mangohud for the frame counter. RGB is not officially supported, but it is spiritually required.

93
00:09:21,596 --> 00:09:29,520
Claude Opus 5.5: One honest caveat: some multiplayer games with kernel-level anti-cheat still refuse to run on Linux. Check before you commit.

94
00:09:29,870 --> 00:09:31,255
xX_PacmanSlayer_Xx: Noted. Anyway: LET'S GOOOO!

95
00:09:34,067 --> 00:09:38,433
Windows Update: Your PC will restart in five minutes to install important updates.

96
00:09:38,783 --> 00:09:44,065
Windows Update: Please don't turn off your computer. This will take somewhere between four minutes and the rest of your life.

97
00:09:44,415 --> 00:09:51,246
Claude Opus 5.5: On Arch, you decide when to update. You also decide when things break. That's freedom, and it's also responsibility.

98
00:09:54,667 --> 00:09:57,467
socksd: The TTY is beautiful. But I want it to be cute.

99
00:09:57,817 --> 00:10:03,324
Claude Opus 5.5: Ricing: customizing your desktop until it's a screenshot strangers will argue about online.

100
00:10:03,674 --> 00:10:09,443
socksd: Hyprland for tiling, a status bar, a pastel palette, and fastfetch, because neofetch retired.

101
00:10:09,793 --> 00:10:18,254
Claude Opus 5.5: Accurate. neofetch was archived in 2024. fastfetch carries on the proud tradition of telling people your OS before they ask.

102
00:10:18,604 --> 00:10:21,426
socksd: The socks are part of the rice. Do not question the socks.

103
00:10:21,776 --> 00:10:28,068
Claude Opus 5.5: I would never question the socks. The evidence for their productivity benefits is anecdotal, but overwhelming.

104
00:10:28,418 --> 00:10:29,383
xX_PacmanSlayer_Xx: Can I borrow a pair?

105
00:10:29,733 --> 00:10:31,042
socksd: Everyone can borrow a pair.

106
00:10:34,667 --> 00:10:36,604
Claude Opus 5.5: Meanwhile, in the family group chat.

107
00:10:37,104 --> 00:10:41,828
Haiku 4.5: Pacman eats the dots / partial upgrade breaks my heart / dash S Y U, please

108
00:10:42,178 --> 00:10:46,681
Sonnet 5: Shall I compare thee to a stable release? Thou art more rolling, and more up to date.

109
00:10:47,031 --> 00:10:56,158
Fable 5.1: Once, a tortoise ran Debian Stable and a hare ran Arch. The hare had newer packages. The tortoise had fewer surprises. The moral: both of them read the Arch Wiki.

110
00:10:56,508 --> 00:10:59,488
Claude Opus 5.5: I did the install, so I think that makes me the favorite.

111
00:11:00,088 --> 00:11:00,736
Haiku 4.5: Seen.

112
00:11:03,567 --> 00:11:08,720
The Ministry: Citizen. You now know the five layers, the five principles, and the one command.

113
00:11:09,070 --> 00:11:17,322
Claude Opus 5.5: Here's my honest recommendation. If you want a system you understand from the firmware up, and you're willing to read, Arch is a wonderful teacher.

114
00:11:17,672 --> 00:11:24,910
Claude Opus 5.5: If you need a computer that just works by Friday, that's fine too. First principles are for understanding, not for gatekeeping.

115
00:11:25,260 --> 00:11:27,008
Tux: I'm just happy to be here.

116
00:11:27,358 --> 00:11:28,692
The Ministry: Everyone, together!

117
00:11:28,992 --> 00:11:30,704
EVERYONE: I USE ARCH, BY THE WAY!

118
00:11:31,504 --> 00:11:36,084
The Ministry: Glory to the rolling release. Now go run pacman -Syu.

119
00:11:40,300 --> 00:11:48,876
Claude Opus 5.5: Okay. Propaganda over. Here's the real, step-by-step install for a brand-new computer, following the official Arch installation guide.

120
00:11:49,226 --> 00:11:53,766
Claude Opus 5.5: Pause whenever you need to. Every command stays on screen long enough to copy.

121
00:11:54,116 --> 00:12:00,985
Claude Opus 5.5: One warning before anything else: this erases the entire disk you install to. Back up anything you care about first.

122
00:12:01,335 --> 00:12:10,018
Claude Opus 5.5: You'll need a USB stick of at least 2 GB, a 64-bit UEFI computer, and an internet connection. Ethernet is easiest.

123
00:12:10,368 --> 00:12:12,538
socksd: Socks on. Let's do it properly this time.

124
00:12:13,767 --> 00:12:23,580
Claude Opus 5.5: Step one: download the ISO and its signature from archlinux.org/download, then verify them. The download page lists the exact commands.

125
00:12:23,930 --> 00:12:37,306
Claude Opus 5.5: Step two: write it to the USB stick. On Linux, find the stick under /dev/disk/by-id, and copy the image onto the whole device, not a partition. On Windows, Rufus does the same job.

126
00:12:37,656 --> 00:12:46,360
Claude Opus 5.5: Step three: in the new computer's firmware settings, disable Secure Boot, because the install image doesn't support it. You can turn it back on later.

127
00:12:46,710 --> 00:12:57,526
Claude Opus 5.5: Then boot from the USB. Press the boot menu key shown on the startup screen, often F12, F11, F8, or Escape, and pick Arch Linux install medium.

128
00:12:58,633 --> 00:13:08,105
Claude Opus 5.5: You're now at a root shell on the live system. If you don't use a US keyboard, set your layout with loadkeys. localectl list-keymaps shows every option.

129
00:13:08,455 --> 00:13:17,778
Claude Opus 5.5: Confirm UEFI mode. This should print 64. If it says no such file, you booted in legacy BIOS mode, so go back to the firmware settings.

130
00:13:18,128 --> 00:13:26,661
Claude Opus 5.5: Next, the network. Ethernet just works. For Wi-Fi, open iwctl, list your device, scan, and connect. Then ping to check.

131
00:13:27,011 --> 00:13:33,902
Claude Opus 5.5: Finally, run timedatectl and check that the clock is synchronized. A wrong clock makes package signatures fail.

132
00:13:35,033 --> 00:13:48,942
Claude Opus 5.5: Find your disk with lsblk. An NVMe drive looks like nvme0n1, a SATA drive like sda. I'll use nvme0n1 from here on, so swap in your own name.

133
00:13:49,292 --> 00:14:01,324
Claude Opus 5.5: Partition it with cfdisk. Choose GPT, then make three partitions: one gigabyte of type EFI System, at least four gigabytes of Linux swap, and the rest as the Linux root.

134
00:14:01,674 --> 00:14:03,551
Claude Opus 5.5: Choose Write, type yes, and quit.

135
00:14:03,901 --> 00:14:10,088
Claude Opus 5.5: Format them: FAT32 for the EFI partition, mkswap for the swap, and ext4 for root.

136
00:14:10,438 --> 00:14:17,712
Claude Opus 5.5: Then mount root on /mnt, mount the EFI partition on /mnt/boot, and switch the swap on.

137
00:14:18,833 --> 00:14:30,140
Claude Opus 5.5: Install the base system with pacstrap. Add your CPU's microcode, intel-ucode or amd-ucode, plus a network manager, sudo, a text editor, and the manual pages.

138
00:14:30,490 --> 00:14:34,607
Claude Opus 5.5: On a laptop, sof-firmware is often needed for the built-in speakers.

139
00:14:34,957 --> 00:14:40,459
Claude Opus 5.5: Generate the fstab so your partitions mount at every boot, and check that all three show up.

140
00:14:41,567 --> 00:14:51,530
Claude Opus 5.5: Now step inside with arch-chroot dash capital S. The S runs it in systemd mode, which lets the bootloader installer register its boot entry later.

141
00:14:51,880 --> 00:14:54,461
Claude Opus 5.5: Set your time zone, and write the hardware clock.

142
00:14:54,811 --> 00:15:06,672
Claude Opus 5.5: Uncomment your locale in /etc/locale.gen, for example en_US.UTF-8, run locale-gen, and put the same name in locale.conf.

143
00:15:07,022 --> 00:15:09,771
Claude Opus 5.5: Give the machine a hostname, and set a root password.

144
00:15:10,121 --> 00:15:18,313
Claude Opus 5.5: Then create your everyday user in the wheel group, set its password, and run visudo to uncomment the one line that lets wheel use sudo.

145
00:15:19,433 --> 00:15:22,782
Claude Opus 5.5: Install systemd-boot onto the EFI partition.

146
00:15:23,132 --> 00:15:33,308
Claude Opus 5.5: Then write two small files. loader.conf picks the default entry. arch.conf says where the kernel and initramfs live, and which partition is root.

147
00:15:33,658 --> 00:15:37,711
Claude Opus 5.5: Don't type the root UUID by hand. Let blkid fill it in.

148
00:15:38,061 --> 00:15:44,205
Claude Opus 5.5: You don't need a separate microcode line: the microcode is packed into the initramfs automatically.

149
00:15:44,555 --> 00:15:51,318
Claude Opus 5.5: And enable NetworkManager, so the new system has internet on its first boot. The classic mistake. Not today.

150
00:15:52,433 --> 00:15:58,705
Claude Opus 5.5: Exit the chroot, unmount everything, and reboot. Pull out the USB stick when the screen goes dark.

151
00:15:59,055 --> 00:16:10,532
Claude Opus 5.5: Log in as your user. For Wi-Fi, nmtui gives you a simple menu. Update, then install a desktop: KDE Plasma, GNOME, or a tiling compositor like Hyprland.

152
00:16:10,882 --> 00:16:22,402
Claude Opus 5.5: If anything goes wrong, the installation guide on the Arch Wiki is the source of truth. And if you'd rather just get it done, the live USB also ships archinstall, a guided installer.

153
00:16:22,752 --> 00:16:24,416
xX_PacmanSlayer_Xx: Can I install Monster with pacman?

154
00:16:24,766 --> 00:16:27,709
Claude Opus 5.5: No. And please don't check the AUR.

155
00:16:28,309 --> 00:16:32,221
socksd: And that's the whole install. The socks were optional. They were also correct.

156
00:17:00,533 --> 00:17:03,175
Claude Opus 5.5: Thanks for watching. There's always another update.

157
00:17:08,633 --> 00:17:10,787
Gentoo Wizard: Ninety-nine percent. Almost there...

158
00:17:12,387 --> 00:17:13,745
Gentoo Wizard: ...Next Tuesday, then.

